<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Cacicazgo of Guaynía</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1511 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The leading Taíno chiefdom of Puerto Rico, in the south of the island, whose caciques Agüeybaná I and II met and then fought the Spanish.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Guaynía occupied the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> (Borikén) around the Guayanilla area. It developed within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> society over the centuries before Spanish arrival and by 1500 was the most powerful of the island's chiefdoms, with other caciques recognising its leader.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its paramount cacique <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agueybana-i">Agüeybaná I</a> welcomed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-ponce-de-leon">Juan Ponce de León</a> in 1508 and allowed the Spanish to settle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> villages centred on ceremonial plazas, such as those at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibes-indigenous-ceremonial-center">Tibes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caguana-ceremonial-site">Caguana</a>, and farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> system the Taíno were forced to work in gold mining.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agueybana-i">Agüeybaná I</a> died around 1510, his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agueybana-ii">Agüeybaná II</a> led a general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> uprising in 1511. It was crushed, Agüeybaná II was probably killed in the fighting, and the Taíno population collapsed under war, forced labour and disease.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1200 CE – 1511 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.58%;width:6.19%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1511 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Agüeybaná I, Agüeybaná II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Taíno religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Taíno</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Guaynía, Agüeybaná's chiefdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1356&amp;focus=cacicazgo-of-guaynia" aria-label="Cacicazgo of Guaynía on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2878 1501 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cacicazgo of Guaynía lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M3209 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3198 1757h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M3198 1757h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Puerto Rico. <a href="/atlas?year=1356&amp;focus=cacicazgo-of-guaynia">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
